WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.519
Rust's safe subset is intentionally
strict.

2
00:00:02.599 --> 00:00:06.426
That strictness is one of the language's
main security strengths because it prevents

3
00:00:06.506 --> 00:00:08.632
many memory-safety mistakes before code
runs.

4
00:00:08.712 --> 00:00:12.060
The compiler asks hard questions about
ownership, borrowing, lifetimes,

5
00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:15.754
aliasing, and mutation so that ordinary
safe code can rely on strong guarantees.

6
00:00:15.834 --> 00:00:19.447
Real systems sometimes need operations the
compiler cannot fully reason about.

7
00:00:19.527 --> 00:00:22.025
A Rust program may need to call an
operating-system API,

8
00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:24.390
interact with embedded hardware, wrap a C
library,

9
00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:27.075
implement a custom allocator, optimize a
data structure,

10
00:00:27.155 --> 00:00:29.440
or work with a platform-specific
representation.

11
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:32.921
These cases can require operations outside
safe Rust's normal guarantees.

12
00:00:33.001 --> 00:00:34.595
Unsafe Rust exists for those cases.

13
00:00:34.675 --> 00:00:37.651
It is a controlled escape hatch, not a
shortcut around discipline.

14
00:00:37.731 --> 00:00:41.452
The unsafe keyword marks places where the
programmer is taking responsibility for

15
00:00:41.532 --> 00:00:43.976
specific assumptions that the compiler
cannot prove.

16
00:00:44.056 --> 00:00:47.829
That signal is valuable because it tells
reviewers where the trust boundary begins.

17
00:00:47.909 --> 00:00:49.716
Unsafe code is not automatically wrong.

18
00:00:49.796 --> 00:00:53.463
Some of Rust's most useful libraries and
platform abstractions rely on carefully

19
00:00:53.543 --> 00:00:54.819
reviewed unsafe internals.

20
00:00:54.899 --> 00:00:58.300
The important question is whether unsafe
is necessary, whether it is small,

21
00:00:58.380 --> 00:00:59.496
whether it is justified,

22
00:00:59.576 --> 00:01:03.030
and whether the surrounding API prevents
ordinary callers from misusing the

23
00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:04.067
low-level behavior.

24
00:01:04.147 --> 00:01:07.973
A professional unsafe Rust practice treats
every unsafe block as something that must

25
00:01:08.053 --> 00:01:08.744
earn its place.

26
00:01:08.824 --> 00:01:11.535
If safe Rust can express the behavior
clearly, use safe Rust.

27
00:01:11.615 --> 00:01:14.803
If unsafe is required, scope it tightly,
document the safety contract,

28
00:01:14.883 --> 00:01:17.700
test boundary behavior, and make review
evidence easy to find.
